{"kind":{"type":"heading","level":2},"runs":[{"text":"Why This Works","style":[],"link":null,"image":false}],"quoteDepth":0,"listDepth":0,"marker":null},
{"kind":{"type":"paragraph"},"runs":[{"text":"NSTableCellView.h:39","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":" states the contract. The table sets the cell's ","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"rowSizeStyle","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":" to its own ","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"effectiveRowSizeStyle","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":", and then \"The cell view will layout the textField and imageView based on the rowSizeStyle.\" Layout here includes the font. A second probe this session showed it: a plain label cell with no constraints got 11, 13, and 15 pt per row size, and its ","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"imageView","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":" was positioned per row size too.","style":[],"link":null,"image":false}],"quoteDepth":0,"listDepth":0,"marker":null},
{"kind":{"type":"paragraph"},"runs":[{"text":"The outlets are the part of the cell AppKit owns. Any per-row styling written to one lasts until the next layout pass. A label that is not an outlet is an ordinary subview: AppKit still forwards ","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"backgroundStyle","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":" to it (","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"NSTableCellView.h:35","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"), so it turns white on a selected row, but it never resets its font. The cell restyles it from two inputs: the row size, which AppKit's reset used, and whether the row is HEAD, which the old code used. Each input's setter re-applies both, so the order in which the table and the delegate set them does not matter.","style":[],"link":null,"image":false}],"quoteDepth":0,"listDepth":0,"marker":null},
{"kind":{"type":"paragraph"},"runs":[{"text":"A table can also keep outlet fonts by setting ","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"rowSizeStyle = .custom","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":", which the probe confirmed. The sidebar does not take that route: its rows follow the system's sidebar size, and ","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":".custom","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":" gives that up.","style":[],"link":null,"image":false}],"quoteDepth":0,"listDepth":0,"marker":null},
{"kind":{"type":"paragraph"},"runs":[{"text":"An attributed string survives because what gets reset is the field's ","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"font","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":" property, and the string's own ","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":".font","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":" attributes win when it draws. This session's probe, with its own strings, kept a monospaced-bold letter and a medium-weight path through layout in both ","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":".automatic","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":" and ","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":".sourceList","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":" styles. That is why ","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"ChangeCellView","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":" can still assign its label to ","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"textField","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":" (","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"mac/Sources/RcGitApp/ChangesViewController.swift:27","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"): it sets ","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"attributedStringValue","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":" (","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"ChangesViewController.swift:777","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"). The ref list's group header also keeps the outlet on purpose (","style":[],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"RefsViewController.swift:409","style":["code"],"link":null,"image":false},{"text":"); there, the source list's own header styling is what is wanted.","style":[],"link":null,"image":false}],"quoteDepth":0,"listDepth":0,"marker":null},
{"kind":{"type":"heading","level":2},"runs":[{"text":"Prevention","style":[],"link":null,"image":false}],"quoteDepth":0,"listDepth":0,"marker":null},
